<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Nothing. This file is deliberately an empty entitlement set, and the
	     emptiness is the point.

	     It used to carry `cs.allow-jit` and `cs.disable-library-validation`,
	     with a comment explaining that the app "spawns a Python subprocess
	     (the render service) and reads arbitrary folders". Every clause of
	     that was true and none of it is: RFC-014 linked a C++ engine into
	     the binary and there is no subprocess, no interpreter, no numba to
	     JIT and no MLX dylibs to load. `allow-jit` was for numba and
	     `disable-library-validation` for MLX; both weaken the hardened
	     runtime that notarisation is about, and a self-contained Metal app
	     needs neither.

	     App Sandbox is off, which is allowed for Developer ID distribution
	     and required only for the Mac App Store. It is now *possible* for
	     the first time — the only thing that read arbitrary paths was the
	     service — but turning it on is not free: `NSOpenPanel` access stops
	     surviving a relaunch, and `Library.chooseFilesOrFolder` would need
	     security-scoped bookmarks, which nothing in the app writes today.

	     `ENABLE_HARDENED_RUNTIME = YES` lives in Tools/gen-project.py.
	     Adding an entitlement back here is adding a hole to it, so say what
	     it is for and what stops working without it. -->
</dict>
</plist>
